          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [

          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [

          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        }
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations per cycle, cutting latency and raising Fmax headroom, but it replicates the datapath k times so luts_plus_ffs grows. It is the second family I am allowed and it adds a distinct trade-off axis (k) that iterative cannot express, so it helps cover the Pareto front where a slightly larger area buys higher accuracy_bits or lower latency at the same throughput. I keep k at its full 2..8 range so the front can show the area penalty of unrolling; n_iter/data_width/guards match iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable on the shared accuracy/area axes."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated (min luts_plus_ffs, selection by min luts_plus_ffs) with a hard throughput floor of 1 MSPS and a hard accuracy floor of max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Both of my assigned families (iterative, unrolled_k) produce one result every several cycles, so the throughput constraint reduces to Fmax >= (N+3)/k MHz, which is comfortably met for the N that satisfy the error bound; the binding constraints are therefore accuracy and area. I split the budget 55/45: iterative gets the larger share because it is the lowest-area family and most likely to contain the selected (min-area) design, while unrolled_k gets the remainder to populate the higher-area / higher-accuracy and lower-latency portion of the front that iterative cannot reach. I leave n_iter, data_width, and both guards at their full registry ranges in the first round so Optuna NSGA-II can discover where the accuracy floor actually binds and where the area/accuracy trade-off curve lies, rather than pre-committing to a narrow region with no prior fleet data. Rounding is left as a 2-value choice since it affects the exact error bits and is cheap to explore."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established and feasible. The minimum area design (iterative, luts_plus_ffs=300) meets the throughput constraint (10.8 MSPS >= 1) and the accuracy constraint (10.6 bits > 10 bits required). The front covers a wide range of accuracy (10.6 to 23.5 bits) and area (300 to 615 LUTs+FFs), providing a good trade-off curve for hypervolume. Since this is the final round, we stop to finalize the design selection based on the 'min luts_plus_ffs' rule, which picks the 300 LUTs+FFs design."
}
